Repeated fixture replay is a demonstrated need in plan review and
intervention review. The adapter matches check identifiers and exact
projected inputs through Rust canonicalization. It does not extend the
evaluator request with case identifiers, labels, or unrelated fields.

Fixture outputs remain synthetic execution controls. Changing examples
to the fixture-test binding generates new profiles; this does not transfer
qualification from a different adapter. Entry validation, snapshot copies,
and missing-match failures prevent silent replacement of test meaning.

The adapter owns no timer or run transition. Existing dispatch, scheduling,
and terminal-state validation remain authoritative. Tests cover reordered,
concurrent, repeated, missing, aborted, and nonportable requests. The full
check passes: 319 Rust unit tests, 37 contracts, 580 TypeScript/repository
checks. Later example and snapshot simplifications pass targeted checks.

Follow-up observed while testing: exhausted retries discard the sanitized
last failure message. Inspect run_state.rs fail_attempt before changing it.
Human usability and comparative effort remain unmeasured.
